        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/62ebf155/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-22\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML file syntax by parsing files specified via command-line arguments. The validator uses a custom-built minimal YAML parser implemented from scratch, ensuring no new external dependencies are added to the project. This tool enables developers to quickly verify that their YAML configuration files are syntactically correct before deployment or further processing.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to validate file syntax without relying on external dependencies. Invalid YAML files can cause runtime errors, deployment failures, or configuration issues that are difficult to debug. Currently, there is no built-in mechanism to validate YAML syntax within the existing toolset.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces debugging time by catching YAML syntax errors early in the development workflow\n- Maintains project simplicity by avoiding additional npm dependencies\n- Provides developers with immediate feedback on configuration file validity\n- Prevents deployment failures caused by malformed YAML files\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Correctly identifies valid YAML files | 100% accuracy for supported syntax |\n| Correctly identifies invalid YAML files | Clear error messaging with line numbers |\n| Command execution time | Less than 500ms for typical config files |\n| No new dependencies added | Zero new npm packages |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|\n| Developer | Team members using CLI tools for development workflow | Quick syntax validation, clear error messages, simple invocation |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain codebase simplicity, no dependency bloat |\n| Project Maintainers | Ensure tool reliability and minimal maintenance burden |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Command-line interface accepting file path argument\n- Custom YAML parser implementation (no external dependencies)\n- Syntax validation (parse success/failure)\n- Clear success/failure output messages\n- Line number reporting for syntax errors\n- Support for common YAML syntax constructs (scalars, mappings, sequences, comments)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (e.g., validating against a JSON Schema)\n- YAML linting or style checking\n- YAML formatting or pretty-printing\n- Multiple file validation in single command\n- Stdin input support\n- YAML anchors and aliases\n- Multi-document YAML files\n- Complex YAML features (tags, custom types, binary data)\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Developers have access to the CLI environment (Bun runtime)\n- YAML files are encoded in UTF-8\n- Target files are single-document YAML files\n- Files are of reasonable size (under 10MB)\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Path Input\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall accept a file path as a command-line argument |\n| Rationale | Developers need to specify which file to validate |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Running `bun run validate config.yaml` passes the file path to the validator |\n\n### REQ-002: File Existence Check\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall verify the specified file exists before attempting validation |\n| Rationale | Clear error messaging prevents confusion when files are missing |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Non-existent file paths produce a clear \"file not found\" error message |\n\n### REQ-003: YAML Syntax Parsing\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall parse YAML content using a custom-built parser without external dependencies |\n| Rationale | Project constraint requires no new npm dependencies |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Parser correctly handles scalars, key-value mappings, lists, nested structures, and comments |\n\n### REQ-004: Success Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall output a success message when the YAML file is syntactically valid |\n| Rationale | Developers need confirmation that validation passed |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Valid YAML files produce output indicating success (e.g., \"Valid YAML\") |\n\n### REQ-005: Error Output with Location\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall output an error message including line number when syntax errors are detected |\n| Rationale | Line numbers help developers quickly locate and fix errors |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Invalid YAML produces error message with line number (e.g., \"Syntax error at line 15: ...\") |\n\n### REQ-006: Exit Codes\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Should Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The command shall return appropriate exit codes (0 for success, non-zero for failure) |\n| Rationale | Enables integration with scripts and CI/CD pipelines |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Exit code 0 on valid YAML, exit code 1 on invalid YAML or errors |\n\n### REQ-007: No New Dependencies\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The implementation shall not add any new npm packages to the project |\n| Rationale | Project constraint to maintain minimal dependencies |\n| Acceptance Criteria | package.json dependencies remain unchanged after implementation |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Requirement | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation time for files under 100KB | Less than 100ms |\n| Validation time for files under 1MB | Less than 500ms |\n| Memory usage | Proportional to file size, no memory leaks |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | Only read access to specified file path |\n| Path traversal | Handle file paths safely without arbitrary file system access |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error clarity | Error messages must be human-readable and actionable |\n| Help text | Command should provide usage information when invoked incorrectly |\n| Output format | Consistent, parseable output format |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNone identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a YAML Configuration File\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Story | As a Developer, I want to validate a YAML configuration file so that I can ensure it will parse correctly before deployment |\n| Acceptance | GIVEN a valid YAML file exists at \"config.yaml\" WHEN I run `bun run validate config.yaml` THEN I see a success message confirming the file is valid |\n\n### STORY-002: Identify Syntax Errors\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Story | As a Developer, I want to see where syntax errors occur so that I can quickly fix malformed YAML |\n| Acceptance | GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error on line 10 WHEN I run the validate command THEN I see an error message indicating the problem at line 10 |\n\n### STORY-003: Handle Missing Files\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Story | As a Developer, I want clear feedback when a file does not exist so that I can correct the file path |\n| Acceptance | GIVEN no file exists at \"missing.yaml\" WHEN I run `bun run validate missing.yaml` THEN I see an error message stating the file was not found |\n\n### STORY-004: Script Integration\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Story | As a Developer, I want the command to return proper exit codes so that I can use it in automated scripts |\n| Acceptance | GIVEN a valid YAML file WHEN the validate command completes THEN the exit code is 0 AND GIVEN an invalid YAML file WHEN the validate command completes THEN the exit code is non-zero |\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The validator must use only built-in language features and existing project code | Maintains zero-dependency constraint |\n| BR-002 | Syntax validation only; no semantic or schema validation | Keeps scope minimal and implementation simple |\n| BR-003 | Single file per invocation | Simplifies implementation and usage |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime | Command executes in Bun environment |\n| File system access | System | Read access to validate target files |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No new npm dependencies | Implementation must use only existing packages and built-in features |\n| Custom parser | YAML parsing must be implemented from scratch |\n| Syntax-only validation | No schema or semantic validation required |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Clarification | Source |\n|----------|---------------|--------|\n| Who are the target users? | Developers via CLI | User clarification |\n| How is input provided? | File path as command argument | User clarification |\n| What level of validation? | Syntax only - check if YAML parses correctly | User clarification |\n| Which YAML parser to use? | Custom minimal parser implemented from scratch | User clarification |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ directory |\n| KB context | Best-effort without KB | index.md and concept_map.md not found |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| Supported YAML features | Basic syntax: scalars, mappings, sequences, comments | Conservative default for \"simple\" validator |\n| Error reporting format | Line number with descriptive message | Standard practice for CLI validators |\n| Exit code convention | 0 for success, 1 for failure | Unix convention default |\n| File size limits | Reasonable files under 10MB | Conservative default assumption |\n| Encoding support | UTF-8 only | Most common YAML encoding |\n| Multi-document support | Out of scope | \"Simple\" validator constraint |\n| Advanced YAML features | Out of scope (anchors, aliases, tags) | Custom minimal parser constraint |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
