        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-01-22\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Parser architecture | Two-phase (lexer + parser) | Cleaner separation of concerns, easier error reporting with line/column tracking, more maintainable code | Single-pass parser (simpler but harder to debug), Regex-based parsing (error-prone for nested structures) |\n| D2 | File structure for CLI | New entry point `src/validate.ts` | Follows existing pattern of `src/index.ts` as entry point, keeps CLI concerns separate from library code | Add to existing `index.ts` (would mix concerns), Subcommand in index.ts (overcomplicates simple project) |\n| D3 | Parser module location | `src/yaml-parser.ts` | Follows existing `src/utils.ts` pattern of utility modules, single-file simplicity | `src/parser/` directory (over-engineered for scope), Inline in validate.ts (poor separation) |\n| D4 | Error location granularity | Line number only | Sufficient for typical YAML debugging, simpler implementation, column tracking adds complexity with minimal benefit | Line + column (more complex), Character offset only (not user-friendly) |\n| D5 | Parser result type | Discriminated union with success field | Type-safe handling of success/error cases, idiomatic TypeScript, clear API contract | Throw exceptions (less explicit control flow), Tuple [data, error] (less type-safe) |\n| D6 | Unsupported YAML features | Return parse error | Clear feedback to users about what went wrong, prevents silent failures or unexpected behavior | Silent ignore (confusing), Partial parse (inconsistent behavior) |\n| D7 | Test file organization | Co-located with source | Matches existing `utils.test.ts` pattern in the project | Separate `__tests__` directory (different from existing pattern), Single test file (poor organization) |\n| D8 | Script registration | Add \"validate\" script to package.json | Consistent with existing scripts pattern, simple invocation via `bun run validate` | Direct `bun src/validate.ts` (verbose), Binary registration (overkill) |\n| D9 | Indentation handling | Spaces only, tabs are errors | YAML spec recommends spaces, simplifies parser, avoids tab/space mixing issues | Support both (complex), Convert tabs to spaces (implicit behavior) |\n| D10 | Multi-line string handling | Support literal (\\|) and folded (>) | Common YAML feature needed for configuration files, reasonable complexity | Skip multi-line (too limiting), Full block scalar spec (over-engineered) |\n\n## Decision Details\n\n### D1: Two-Phase Parser Architecture\n\n**Context**: Need to implement a custom YAML parser without external dependencies.\n\n**Decision**: Implement parser in two phases - a lexer (tokenizer) that converts input to tokens, followed by a parser that builds the AST from tokens.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: Clear separation between lexical and syntactic analysis\n- Positive: Error messages can include precise location information\n- Positive: Easier to test each phase independently\n- Negative: Slightly more code than a single-pass approach\n- Negative: Two passes over the input (minimal performance impact for typical files)\n\n### D3: Single-File Parser Module\n\n**Context**: How to organize the YAML parser code.\n\n**Decision**: Keep all parser code in a single file `src/yaml-parser.ts`.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: Matches existing project pattern (utils.ts)\n- Positive: Simple to understand and navigate\n- Positive: No complex module dependencies\n- Negative: File may grow large for complex parsers\n- Mitigation: Scope limited to basic YAML features keeps file manageable\n\n### D5: Discriminated Union Result Type\n\n**Context**: How to communicate parse success/failure to callers.\n\n**Decision**: Use TypeScript discriminated union with `success: boolean` discriminator.\n\n```typescript\ntype YamlParseResult =\n  | { success: true; data: YamlValue }\n  | { success: false; error: string; line: number };\n```\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: Type-safe - compiler ensures both cases handled\n- Positive: No exceptions for expected failure cases\n- Positive: Error information always available on failure\n- Negative: Slightly more verbose than try/catch\n\n### D9: Spaces-Only Indentation\n\n**Context**: YAML allows both spaces and tabs, but mixing causes issues.\n\n**Decision**: Only allow spaces for indentation. Tabs produce a parse error.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: Simplifies parser implementation\n- Positive: Consistent with YAML best practices\n- Positive: Avoids tab/space mixing bugs\n- Negative: Files using tabs will fail (rare in practice)\n"
