        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-22\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML file syntax and structure, enabling users to verify their YAML files are well-formed before use in configuration, deployment, or data exchange scenarios. The validator must be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies to the project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML files need a quick way to validate syntax correctness without relying on external tools or services. Invalid YAML files can cause deployment failures, configuration errors, and debugging time waste. Currently, there is no built-in validation capability in the project.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging malformed YAML files\n- Prevents deployment and configuration failures caused by syntax errors\n- Provides immediate feedback during development workflows\n- Maintains project simplicity by avoiding dependency bloat\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | Correctly identifies 100% of YAML syntax errors |\n| Response time | Validates files under 1MB in less than 1 second |\n| Zero new dependencies | No additions to package.json dependencies |\n| User adoption | Command is used in CI/CD or pre-commit workflows |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|\n| Developer | Team members creating or editing YAML files | Quick syntax validation, clear error messages |\n| DevOps Engineer | Personnel managing configuration files | Batch validation, CI/CD integration |\n| QA Engineer | Testing team members | Validation as part of test suites |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep dependency count minimal, maintain code simplicity |\n| End Users | Reliable, fast validation with actionable error messages |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Validate YAML syntax correctness for single files\n- Validate YAML syntax correctness for multiple files (batch mode)\n- Report line numbers and positions of syntax errors\n- Support standard YAML 1.1/1.2 syntax\n- Command-line interface accessible via project scripts\n- Exit codes suitable for CI/CD integration (0 for success, non-zero for failure)\n- Human-readable error messages\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating against a specific YAML schema)\n- YAML file repair or auto-correction\n- YAML formatting or prettifying\n- YAML to JSON or other format conversion\n- GUI or web-based interface\n- Remote file validation (URLs)\n- Streaming validation for very large files\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime provides native YAML parsing capabilities or JavaScript-based parsing is sufficient\n- Target YAML files are local filesystem files\n- Files are encoded in UTF-8\n- Maximum practical file size is under 10MB\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Single File Validation\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The system shall accept a file path as input and validate the YAML syntax of that file.\n**Rationale**: Core functionality needed for basic validation use case.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given a valid YAML file path, when validation is run, then success message is displayed and exit code is 0\n- Given an invalid YAML file path, when validation is run, then error message with file path is displayed and exit code is non-zero\n- Given a file with invalid YAML syntax, when validation is run, then error details including line number are displayed\n\n### REQ-002: Multiple File Validation\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The system shall accept multiple file paths and validate each file, reporting results for all files.\n**Rationale**: Batch validation improves efficiency for users managing multiple configuration files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given multiple file paths, when validation is run, then each file is validated independently\n- Given a mix of valid and invalid files, when validation is run, then all results are reported and exit code reflects overall status\n- Validation continues through all files even if some fail\n\n### REQ-003: Error Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: All Users\n**Requirement**: The system shall report syntax errors with file name, line number, column position (if available), and a description of the error.\n**Rationale**: Detailed error information enables quick identification and resolution of issues.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Error messages include the file path\n- Error messages include the line number where the error was detected\n- Error messages include a human-readable description of the syntax problem\n- Error output is written to stderr\n\n### REQ-004: Exit Code Behavior\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The system shall return exit code 0 when all validated files are valid, and a non-zero exit code when any file is invalid or an error occurs.\n**Rationale**: Standard exit codes enable integration with CI/CD pipelines and shell scripts.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code 0 when all files pass validation\n- Exit code 1 when one or more files fail validation\n- Exit code 2 when command-line arguments are invalid or files cannot be read\n\n### REQ-005: Glob Pattern Support\n**Priority**: Could Have\n**User Type**: DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The system should accept glob patterns to match multiple YAML files for validation.\n**Rationale**: Simplifies validation of all YAML files in a directory structure.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given a glob pattern like \"config/*.yaml\", when validation is run, then all matching files are validated\n- Given a pattern matching no files, when validation is run, then appropriate message is displayed\n\n### REQ-006: Quiet Mode\n**Priority**: Could Have\n**User Type**: DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The system should support a quiet mode that only outputs errors, suppressing success messages.\n**Rationale**: Cleaner output for CI/CD logs and scripted usage.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given quiet flag is set and files are valid, when validation is run, then no output is produced\n- Given quiet flag is set and files are invalid, when validation is run, then only error messages are output\n\n### REQ-007: No New Dependencies\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Project Maintainers\n**Requirement**: The implementation shall not add any new entries to package.json dependencies or devDependencies beyond what currently exists.\n**Rationale**: Explicit constraint from requirements to maintain project simplicity.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- package.json dependencies section remains unchanged after implementation\n- package.json devDependencies section remains unchanged after implementation\n- All YAML parsing is done using built-in capabilities or inline code\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Metric | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Small files (<100KB) | Validation completes in under 100ms |\n| Medium files (100KB-1MB) | Validation completes in under 1 second |\n| Large files (1MB-10MB) | Validation completes in under 10 seconds |\n| Memory usage | Should not exceed 100MB for files under 10MB |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- File paths must be validated to prevent path traversal attacks\n- Only local filesystem files should be accessible\n- No execution of content within YAML files\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- Error messages should be clear and actionable\n- Command syntax should follow common CLI conventions\n- Help text should be available via --help flag\n- Command should be runnable via npm/bun scripts\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- None identified for this feature\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Basic Validation\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate a YAML configuration file\n**So that** I can ensure it has correct syntax before using it\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file path\n- WHEN I run the yaml validator command with the file path\n- THEN I see a success message if valid, or detailed error information if invalid\n\n### STORY-002: Pre-commit Validation\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate all YAML files in my project\n**So that** I can catch syntax errors before committing code\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN multiple YAML file paths or a glob pattern\n- WHEN I run the yaml validator command\n- THEN all files are checked and I see a summary of results\n\n### STORY-003: CI Pipeline Integration\n**As a** DevOps engineer\n**I want** the validator to return proper exit codes\n**So that** my CI pipeline can fail builds on invalid YAML files\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN YAML files in a CI pipeline\n- WHEN the validator runs\n- THEN exit code 0 indicates success, non-zero indicates failure\n\n### STORY-004: Debugging Invalid Files\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to see line numbers in error messages\n**So that** I can quickly locate and fix syntax errors\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error\n- WHEN I run the validator\n- THEN the error message includes the line number and error description\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | Files with .yaml or .yml extensions are treated as YAML files | Standard YAML file extensions |\n| BR-002 | Empty files are considered valid YAML | Empty document is valid per YAML spec |\n| BR-003 | Files that cannot be read produce file access errors, not validation errors | Distinguishes between access issues and syntax issues |\n| BR-004 | Multi-document YAML files (using ---) are supported | Common pattern in Kubernetes and other configs |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun Runtime | Runtime | Project uses Bun as the JavaScript runtime |\n| Filesystem Access | System | Requires read access to target YAML files |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Impact |\n|------------|--------|\n| No new npm dependencies | Must use built-in parsing or implement YAML parsing inline |\n| TypeScript codebase | Implementation should be in TypeScript consistent with project |\n| Bun-specific APIs allowed | May use Bun built-in capabilities if available |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What YAML versions to support? | YAML 1.1 and 1.2 (standard) | Conservative default |\n| Should schema validation be included? | No, syntax validation only | Scope constraint for simplicity |\n| Maximum file size? | 10MB practical limit | Conservative default for CLI tool |\n| Support for stdin input? | Out of scope for initial version | MVP scoping |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None available | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ |\n| KB context | Best-effort without KB | KB files (index.md, concept_map.md) not present |\n| Project type | TypeScript/Bun CLI project | Inferred from package.json and src/ structure |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" validator scope | Syntax validation only, no schema validation | Conservative interpretation of \"simple\" |\n| \"command\" interface | CLI command runnable via bun/npm scripts | Project structure shows CLI pattern in index.ts |\n| \"without adding new deps\" | Zero additions to dependencies or devDependencies | Literal interpretation of constraint |\n| Target users | Developers and DevOps engineers | Standard users for CLI validation tools |\n| Error verbosity | Include line numbers and descriptions | Industry standard for validators |\n| Multi-file support | Should Have priority | Common need but not explicitly required |\n| YAML version support | 1.1 and 1.2 | Most common versions in use |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
