ng)\n   - Edge cases are numerous and require full specification compliance\n   - Simple regex/line-by-line approaches cannot maintain required state\n\n3. **Recommended Approach**:\n   - **Option A (Preferred)**: Use js-yaml (~35KB minified) as the validation engine. It's lightweight, well-tested, and handles YAML 1.2.\n   - **Option B (Hybrid)**: Use inline checks for immediate feedback on obvious errors (unclosed quotes, unbalanced braces) PLUS js-yaml for full validation.\n   - **Option C (Bundle)**: If zero-dependency is critical, consider bundling a minimal YAML parser subset, but this requires significant effort and testing.\n\n4. **Performance Note**: From research, js-yaml is already considered lightweight and fast. The trade-off of ~35KB for reliable YAML validation is favorable versus implementing custom parsing.\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001 | HIGH | REJECTED | Inline YAML validation insufficient; use js-yaml or similar library |\n"
