        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-01-22T00:00:00Z | **Status**: VALIDATED\n\n## Hypotheses\n### HYP-001: Lightweight Inline YAML Syntax Validator\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: REJECTED\n**Statement**: A lightweight inline YAML syntax validator can accurately detect common syntax errors for YAML 1.1/1.2 without relying on external libraries.\n**Context**: The feature requires validating YAML syntax without adding external dependencies. This hypothesis tests whether a custom inline parser can effectively catch common errors.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: Prototype inline parser correctly identifies syntax errors in sample YAML files including: invalid indentation, unterminated strings, malformed keys, multi-document markers\n- REJECT if: Prototype fails to detect common syntax errors or produces false positives on valid YAML that js-yaml/other parsers accept\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-01-22T00:00:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT + EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n**Result**: REJECTED\n\n**Evidence**:\n\n#### Experiment 1: Initial Inline Validator Prototype\nA TypeScript inline YAML validator was implemented with the following capabilities:\n- Tab character detection\n- Quote balance checking (single and double quotes)\n- Flow context tracking (braces/brackets)\n- Indentation stack management\n- Document marker handling (---, ...)\n- Multiline string block support (|, >)\n\n**Initial Results**: 14/15 tests passed (93.3%), 4/4 edge cases passed (94.7% overall)\n\n**Critical Failure**: Failed to detect subtle indentation inconsistency:\n```yaml\nperson:\n  name: John\n   age: 30  # 3 spaces instead of 2 - NOT DETECTED\n```\n\n#### Experiment 2: Enhanced Indentation Detection\nEnhanced the validator with:\n- Indent unit tracking for consistent increments\n- Improved dedent validation against indent stack\n\n**Results**: 9/11 tests passed (81.8%)\n\n**Still Failed**:\n- Inconsistent indentation (3 spaces after 2)\n- Odd spaces in even-space file patterns\n\n#### Experiment 3: Comparison with js-yaml\nDirect comparison testing against js-yaml library across 11 test cases:\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|--------|-------|\n| Both catch error | 1 |\n| Only js-yaml catches (inline MISSES) | 8 |\n| Inline false positives | 0 |\n| Both say valid | 2 |\n\n**Inline Validator Effectiveness: 11.1%** of errors that js-yaml catches\n\n**Errors Inline Validator MISSED**:\n1. Bad indentation of mapping entry (subtle spacing)\n2. Duplicate keys in same mapping\n3. Unidentified alias references (*undefined_anchor)\n4. Tab indentation errors (special char parsing needed)\n5. Invalid mapping syntax (key: : value)\n6. Trailing content after flow mapping\n7. Bad indentation in list items\n8. Mixed indentation styles\n\n**Errors Inline Validator CAN Detect**:\n1. Unterminated quoted strings (quote counting)\n2. Unclosed braces/brackets (balance counting)\n3. Explicit tab checks (with proper escaping)\n\n**Sources**:\n- Experimental code: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator/inline-yaml-validator.ts`\n- Experimental code: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator/enhanced-validator.ts`\n- Comparison script: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator/compare-with-jsyaml.js`\n- js-yaml library: https://github.com/nodeca/js-yaml\n- YAML 1.2 Specification: https://yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html\n- Symfony YAML Component docs: https://symfony.com/doc/current/components/yaml.html\n- YAML Checker: https://yamlchecker.com/\n\n**Implications for Design**:\n\n1. **Primary Finding**: A lightweight inline YAML validator CANNOT reliably detect common syntax errors. The inline approach only catches approximately 11% of errors that a full parser like js-yaml detects.\n\n2. **Why Inline Fails**:\n   - YAML parsing is context-sensitive (indentation depends on parent level)\n   - Semantic validation requires building data structures (duplicate keys, anchor tracking)\n   - Edge cases are numerous and require full specification compliance\n   - Simple regex/line-by-line approaches cannot maintain required state\n\n3. **Recommended Approach**:\n   - **Option A (Preferred)**: Use js-yaml (~35KB minified) as the validation engine. It's lightweight, well-tested, and handles YAML 1.2.\n   - **Option B (Hybrid)**: Use inline checks for immediate feedback on obvious errors (unclosed quotes, unbalanced braces) PLUS js-yaml for full validation.\n   - **Option C (Bundle)**: If zero-dependency is critical, consider bundling a minimal YAML parser subset, but this requires significant effort and testing.\n\n4. **Performance Note**: From research, js-yaml is already considered lightweight and fast. The trade-off of ~35KB for reliable YAML validation is favorable versus implementing custom parsing.\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001 | HIGH | REJECTED | Inline YAML validation insufficient; use js-yaml or similar library |\n"
